In the $U_1$ leptoquark model, the authors re-examine the calculation of the branching ratio of the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay and find it to be significantly influenced by the recent observations of $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions. Unlike other leptoquark models, the $U_1$ model predicts a much higher ratio of $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ to $\Lambda_b 	o 	au$ decay modes. This distinction arises due to the contribution of the operator $O_5$, which in the $U_1$ model causes a shift in the flavor changing long-distance contribution, resulting in a higher branching ratio of $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$. A precise measurement of this ratio by LHCb could serve as a signature in identifying the $B$-violating leptoquark, given that other models, which do not exhibit this ratio enhancement, are ruled out.
